  A minimal table with chaining fits in a few lines of Python:

  [0m[38;5;4mclass[0m [0m[38;5;4mTable[0m:
      [0m[38;5;4mdef [0m[38;5;3m__init__[0m(self, size=[0m[38;5;2m8[0m):
          self.buckets = [[] [0m[38;5;4mfor [0m_ [0m[38;5;4min [0m[38;5;6mrange[0m(size)]

      [0m[38;5;4mdef [0m[38;5;3mput[0m(self, key, value):
          bucket = self.buckets[[0m[38;5;6mhash[0m(key) % [0m[38;5;6mlen[0m(self.buckets)]
          [0m[38;5;4mfor [0mi, (k, _) [0m[38;5;4min [0m[38;5;6menumerate[0m(bucket):
              [0m[38;5;4mif [0mk == key:
                  bucket[i] = (key, value)
                  [0m[38;5;4mreturn[0m
          bucket.append((key, value))

      [0m[38;5;4mdef [0m[38;5;3mget[0m(self, key):
          bucket = self.buckets[[0m[38;5;6mhash[0m(key) % [0m[38;5;6mlen[0m(self.buckets)]
          [0m[38;5;4mfor [0mk, v [0m[38;5;4min [0mbucket:
              [0m[38;5;4mif [0mk == key:
                  [0m[38;5;4mreturn[0m v
          [0m[38;5;4mraise [0mKeyError(key)

  Speed depends on the [0m[1mload factor[0m, the number of entries divided by the number of buckets. As the factor rises,
  collisions multiply and lookups slow. Most implementations therefore resize once the factor passes a threshold, often
  0.75: they allocate an array twice as large and reinsert every entry. A single resize is costly, but doublings are
  rare, so the [0m[1mamortised [0mcost of an insert stays constant.

  The worst case is grim. If every key lands in one bucket, a lookup degrades to a linear scan. Attackers have
  exploited this by sending web servers keys crafted to collide, which is why Python and other languages randomise
  their string hashes at start-up. Keys must also keep the same hash while stored, which is why Python allows tuples as
  dictionary keys but not lists.

